\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Takes Actions On Portuguese Banks After Portugal Upgrade\b0\par\par\uc2 4,138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2022\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 16 Sep 2022 07:51 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Takes Actions On Portuguese Banks After Portugal Upgrade\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- On Sept. 9, 2022, S&P Global Ratings raised its long-term sovereign \par credit rating on Portugal to 'BBB+' on solid fiscal and growth outcomes, and \par kept the outlook stable. \par      -- Portuguese banks have made substantial progress in rebalancing their \par funding profiles, making them less risky. \par      -- We have therefore raised our long-term ratings on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Santander \par Totta\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Comercial Portugues\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and kept the outlooks on \par these banks stable. \par      -- We affirmed our ratings on Haitong Bank and maintained the stable \par outlook. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par MADRID (S&P Global Ratings) Sept. 16, 2022--S&P Global Ratings today took the \par following rating actions on Portuguese banks (see the Ratings List for full \par details): \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Santander Totta S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  .: We raised our long-term issuer credit \par rating by one notch to 'BBB+' from 'BBB' and affirmed the 'A-2' short-term \par rating. The outlook is stable. \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco BPI S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  .: We raised our long-term issuer credit rating by one \par notch to 'BBB+' from 'BBB' and affirmed the 'A-2' short-term rating. The \par outlook is stable. \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Comercial Portugues S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  .: We raised our long-term issuer credit \par ratings by one notch to 'BB+' from 'BB' and affirmed the 'B' short-term \par rating. The outlook is stable. \par      -- Haitong Bank S.A.: We affirmed our 'BB/B' long- and short-term issuer \par credit ratings and maintained our stable outlook. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par Despite higher energy costs and rising interest rates, Portugal has continued \par to post strong growth, employment figures, and fiscal outcomes. \par Investment is set to surge on the back of an expected EUR61.2 billion (26% of \par GDP) in EU funding between 2022 and 2027. Meanwhile, the government's \par single-party majority reduces uncertainty about the implementation of fiscal \par and structural reforms, while at the same time we expect Portugal's budget to \par operate a primary surplus (excluding interest payments) already this year, \par putting its debt to GDP on a steeper downward path than peers'. (see "Portugal \par Upgraded To 'BBB+' On Solid Fiscal And Growth Outcomes, Anchored By EU \par Inflows; Outlook Stable," published Sept. 9, 2022, on RatingsDirect). \par  \par Asset quality deterioration will likely become more evident in the coming \par quarters, but should be manageable for Portuguese banks. \par Despite the pandemic, problem loans declined in Portugal over 2020-2021 and in \par the first few months of 2022, as banks continued to work out legacy exposures, \par although significant differences persist among the largest players. We expect \par problem loans to rise by the end of this year and next, due to the effects of \par higher inflation, slower economic activity, and tighter financial conditions \par on borrowers, but the impact should be manageable for banks as long as the \par economy continues growing. In particular, we forecast that the domestic \par banking sector's problem loans ratio could be close to 6.0% by end-2023 \par (estimated 5.6% at end-2021), with the domestic cost of risk standing at \par around 65 basis points (bps), from an estimated 41 bps in 2021. Problems will \par concentrate most likely among those borrowers with pre-existing weaknesses \par operating in industries affected by higher energy and commodity prices, such \par as accommodation, food services, transportation, and storage. \par  \par Meanwhile, we expect the housing market to remain relatively dynamic. \par We anticipate that nominal house prices will appreciate more slowly, compared \par with 11.6% growth in 2021 and more than 9% average annual growth in 2017-2020. \par Housing supply shortages and nonresidents' demand for housing have been \par contributing to price increases. The share of residential real estate \par transactions financed by domestic credit remains much lower than during the \par last financial crisis, at around 55% in 2021, compared with 76% in 2009. At \par the same time, the adoption of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Portugal\f28 \uc2 \f2  's macroprudential \par recommendations on new credit agreements introduced in 2018 has led to an \par improvement in borrowers' risk profiles and in the characteristics of the \par housing loan portfolio. These factors, along with our expectation that foreign \par demand will continue sustaining the housing market, suggest that even if there \par were to be a price correction, the performance of banks' mortgage portfolios \par would be fairly resilient. \par  \par Banks' profitability will continue improving, but prospects remain weaker than \par peers'. \par We expect rising interest rates to benefit the Portuguese banking system over \par the next 24 months, although controlling the repricing of deposits could prove \par challenging, and higher inflation will put pressure on operating costs and \par credit costs. We forecast a return on equity (ROE) of about 5.5% in 2022-2023, \par which is an improvement compared with the 3% average over the past five years. \par It will still be below banks' cost of capital and weaker than the ROE of \par stronger banking systems in peer countries. In addition, we anticipate that \par Portuguese banks will remain efficient, with the system's cost-to-income ratio \par at around 52%. \par  \par We see lower systemwide funding risks for banks. \par Portuguese banks have significantly rebalanced their funding profiles over the \par past decade. Deposits account for the bulk of their funding structures, and \par exceed their loan books, with the system's loan-to-deposit ratio at below 80%, \par according to European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Supervisory Statistics, down from more than \par 130% a decade ago. In 2021, customer deposits grew by almost 9% year on year. \par Historically, deposits have proven fairly stable, even in difficult times. In \par addition, Portuguese banks' reliance on external debt is now much more limited \par than a decade ago. Banks have only occasionally tapped the foreign debt \par markets to build up their minimum requirement for own funds and eligible \par liabilities in recent years. Lastly, we believe that, while they accessed \par targeted longer-term refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (TLTRO) facilities to a \par significant amount (total outstanding EUR22 billion as of end-2021, or around \par 10% of total liabilities, according to our calculations), Portuguese banks \par have the capacity to repay upcoming TLTRO maturities in 2023-2024 with \par existing liquidity buffers, and still maintain comfortable regulatory ratios. \par In particular, we estimate that the liquidity coverage ratio and net stable \par funding ratio of Portugal's banking system will stand at around 240% and 131%, \par respectively, after full TLTRO repayment (see "The New Normal For Eurozone \par Banks: Strong Funding Franchises Are Back In Vogue," published July 7, 2022). \par  \par We now see reduced industry risks for Portuguese banks. \par Our view of lower systemwide funding risks for Portuguese banks has led us to \par revise our industry risk score to '5' from '6', and we now observe a stable \par industry risk trend whereas it was positive previously. Our economic risk \par score remains '6', and we still see a stable trend for economic risk. We now \par assign a stronger banking industry country risk assessment of '5' to Portugal, \par versus '6' before. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BANCO SANTANDER TOTTA S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par Primary credit analyst: Miriam Fernandez \par Rationale \par The upgrade follows a similar action on Portugal, as we can now include in \par Totta's rating one additional notch of group support. The rating on Totta, \par however, remains capped at the level of the sovereign rating, because we do \par not consider the parent willing to support its subsidiary during stress \par associated with a sovereign default. We continue to consider Totta as a \par strategically important subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Santander S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  . and we continue to \par apply a cap on our long-term rating on Totta at one notch below the \par unsupported group credit profile on Santander. We revised up our assessment of \par Totta 's stand-alone credit profile to 'bbb' from 'bbb-', following the \par improvement of the starting point for banks operating in Portugal (the \par anchor). \par  \par We expect Totta will maintain its solid franchise in Portugal and \par better-than-peers' efficiency. The cost-to-income ratio should stand about \par 44%-46% in the next 18-24 months. We think that the bank is well placed to \par face the toughening economic environment and that its returns will gradually \par recover to pre-pandemic levels. \par  \par Outlook \par Our stable outlook on Totta mirrors that on Portugal. Any changes in the \par sovereign rating will lead to a similar action on Totta, all other things \par being equal. \par  \par Upside scenario: \par We could upgrade Totta following a similar action on the sovereign in the next \par 18-24 months, assuming Totta remains a strategically important subsidiary of \par Santander. This is because, owing to its strategic importance, we could still \par apply one additional notch of group support to our rating on Totta if the \par sovereign rating constraint did not exist. \par  \par Downside scenario: \par We could lower our rating on Totta in the next 18-24 months if we were to \par lower the rating on Portugal, since at the current rating level, the long-term \par sovereign credit rating on Portugal continues to constrain our rating on \par Totta. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BANCO BPI S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par Primary credit analyst: Miriam Fernandez \par Rationale \par The upgrade reflects a similar action on Portugal, as we can now include one \par additional notch of group support from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2   into our rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  . We \par continue incorporating two notches of group support to the rating, which \par reflects that we see \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2   as a highly strategic subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par (A-/Stable/A-2). At 'BBB+', our rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2   now stands one-notch below the \par long-term rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par We revised up our assessment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  's stand-alone credit profile, reflecting \par easing industry risks for banks operating in Portugal, as well as the \par sustainable enhancement of \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  's capitalization. We now forecast our \par risk-adjusted capital (\f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) ratio will stand sustainably above 7%, supported \par by the positive rating action on the sovereign, which has a positive 45-bps \par impact on our \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio. Thus, we forecast the \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio will be 7.9% by \par end-2022, and modestly decline thereafter on the back of high dividend \par payments to parent \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par Outlook \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Sep 2022 07:51 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Takes Actions On Portuguese -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Our stable outlook mirrors that on Portugal. It also reflects our expectation \par that \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2   will remain a highly strategic subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and that its \par capitalization will remain adequate for the risks it bears, with our \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio \par standing sustainably above 7% over the next 18-24 months. \par  \par Upside scenario: \par An upgrade is highly unlikely because we are including as much extraordinary \par support as possible from the parent, given \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  's group status and the current \par rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . However, we could consider raising the rating if we \par raised both the rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the sovereign rating. \par  \par Downside scenario: \par We could lower our rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2   in the next 18-24 months if we lowered the \par rating on Portugal. Alternatively, we could also lower the rating if we \par lowered the long-term rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caixabank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , since we cap the rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2   at \par one-notch below the rating on the parent. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BANCO COMERCIAL PORTUGUES S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par Primary credit analyst: Miriam Fernandez \par Rationale \par The upgrade reflects that \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's credit risk profile has gradually improved in \par absolute terms and relative to peers' amid easing industry risks for the \par Portuguese system, although its capitalization remains a ratings weakness. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par has reduced its stock of nonperforming loans (NPLs) by about 40% since \par end-2019 and its NPLs stood at a manageable 4.3% of its gross loans at \par end-June 2022, with a 65% coverage as of the same date. Its asset quality \par metrics have closed the gap with those of the Portuguese banking system and \par its peer group. The rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2   also reflects potential downside risks \par arising from the group's Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . While we expect higher legal and \par credit losses in Poland, credit risks should remain overall contained. Also in \par a rising interest rate environment, the risk of the bank's capital being hit \par by its pension obligations to its employees has also reduced significantly. \par  \par We still consider that \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's capitalization remains modest for the risks it \par faces. We estimate that our \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio will hover around 6.5%-7.0% over the \par next 12-18 months, a level that is comparatively lower than that of higher \par rated peers. \par  \par We affirmed the 'CCC+' rating on the additional tier-1 (AT1) instrument. This \par is because we now deduct one additional notch that reflects a tight maximum \par distributable amount (MDA) buffer. At end-June 2022, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's MDA headroom on its \par AT1 stood at 147 bps, with its Tier 1 capital ratio standing at 12.4%. Its MDA \par buffer would stand at 180 bps pro forma for the exclusion of structural \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positions held to hedge capital ratios. \par  \par Outlook \par The stable outlook reflects our expectation that the bank should be able to \par defend its solid retail franchise in Portugal and capitalization over the next \par 12-18 months, despite the secondary effects from the military conflict in \par Ukraine. We expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2   will gradually further strengthen its domestic \par profitability, while maintaining better-than-peers' efficiency. \par  \par While there could be further unpredictable market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , we \par anticipate that the extraordinary costs on its subsidiary will have a \par manageable effect on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's capitalization and capital will remain moderate. \par  \par Downside scenario: \par We could lower the rating if \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's litigation risks and extraordinary costs in \par Poland become worse than we currently expect, impacting \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's consolidated \par capital position or its risk profile more adversely than we expect. \par  \par Additionally, we could lower the rating on the AT1 instrument if, contrary to \par our base-case expectation, the bank's MDA buffer fell sustainably below 100 \par bps. This could happen due to further negative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Polish \par government that could hit more meaningfully the subsidiary and thus \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2  's \par capital, and the bank was unable to activate some of its capital levers. \par Equally, tighter available distributable item buffers could put pressure on \par the rating, although at end-2021 these covered coupon payments by more than \par 12x. \par  \par Upside scenario: \par Although unlikely in the next 12-18 months due to headwinds on its Polish \par business, we could consider raising the rating if the capitalization \par sustainably strengthened above the 7% threshold, and the bank's performance in \par maintaining its efficiency and earnings generation capacity improves. An \par upgrade would also be contingent on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BCP\f28 \uc2 \f2   absorbing the one-off costs of its \par Polish business, with any potential additional detrimental measures by the \par Polish government having a limited effect on the bank. \par  \par HAITONG BANK S.A. \par Primary analyst: Anais Ozyavuz \par Rationale \par The affirmation reflects our view that the lower systemwide funding risks we \par see for Portuguese banks does not directly impact Haitong Bank. In particular, \par the bank's funding profile remains comparatively weaker than its peers' \par because of its higher reliance on wholesale funding (over 60% of total funding \par at end-June 2022), while its deposits base is concentrated and price \par sensitive. At the same time, even if Haitong Bank proved resilient amid the \par pandemic and has sharply worked out its legacy NPLs over the last few years, \par it has not been able to benefit from management's continued efforts to \par strengthen the domestic franchise and develop its cross-border activity with \par Chinese customers. In fact, the bank returned to losses in the first half of \par 2022, mostly explained by the China-related business and the COVID-related \par lockdowns in several cities in the country. \par  \par Our rating on Haitong Bank continues to reflect its lack of scale in the \par investment banking industry, and its comparatively lower efficiency and \par profitability when compared with domestic and international peers. This leaves \par it with a reduced loss absorption capacity. In addition, its high single-name \par concentration and high exposure to lower-rated companies weigh on its risk \par profile, in our view, along with its comparatively weaker funding profile. At \par the same time, our long-term issuer credit rating on Haitong Bank continues to \par factor in three notches of uplift for extraordinary parental support. This is \par because we consider Haitong Bank a strategically important subsidiary of its \par China-based parent, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haitong Securities Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . (BBB/Stable/A-2). \par Outlook \par The stable outlook on Haitong Bank reflects our expectation that the bank will \par remain focused on strengthening its business model, even if we don't \par anticipate a sustainable profitability improvement. We also expect organic \par capital generation will be modest, but sufficient to preserve the bank's \par enhanced capital position, with our \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio standing somewhat above 10% over \par the next 12-18 months. \par  \par Upside scenario: \par We could raise our rating on Haitong Bank if it improved its operating \par profitability sustainably, generating recurring and stable revenue, while \par gradually aligning its efficiency with peers'. Although not our base case, we \par could also raise the rating if we considered Haitong Bank to be a more \par important subsidiary for its Chinese parent. \par  \par Downside scenario: \par We could lower our rating if the bank were to incur material losses that \par pushed its RAC ratio below 10%, especially if its exposure to riskier asset \par classes increased substantially, resulting in much higher impairment losses. \par We could also lower the rating if the bank faced a renewed need to restructure \par so as to strengthen its domestic franchise and improve its operating \par efficiency, or if the parent's commitment to the bank faltered. \par  \par BICRA SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par Portugal \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Hybrid Capital: Methodology And Assumptions, March \par 2, 2022 \par      -- Criteria | Financial Institutions | General: Financial Institutions \par Rating Methodology, Dec. 9, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Financial Institutions | Banks: Banking Industry Country \par Risk Assessment Methodology And Assumptions, Dec. 9, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Group Rating Methodology, July 1, 2019 \par      -- Criteria | Financial Institutions | General: Risk-Adjusted Capital \par Framework Methodology, July 20, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Guarantee Criteria, Oct. 21, 2016 \par      -- General Criteria: Criteria For Assigning 'CCC+', '\f28 \uc2 \f2 CCC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ', 'CCC -', And \par 'CC' Ratings, Oct. 1, 2012 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Portugal Upgraded To 'BBB+' On Solid Fiscal And Growth Outcomes, \par Anchored By EU Inflows; Outlook Stable, Sept. 9, 2022 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment Update: August 2022, Aug. 30, \par 2022 \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Santander Totta\f28 \uc2 \f2  , Aug. 30, 2022 \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Comercial Portugues\f28 \uc2 \f2   'BB/B' Ratings Affirmed On Resilience To \par Capital Hit At Subsidiary; Outlook Remains Stable, July 21, 2022 \par      -- The New Normal For Eurozone Banks: Strong Funding Franchises Are Back \par In Vogue, July 7, 2022 \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Comercial Portugues\f28 \uc2 \f2  , May 25, 2022 \par      -- Haitong Bank, Feb. 23, 2022 \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco BPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  , April 26, 2021 \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at www.standardandpoors.com for further \par information. A description of each of S&P Global Ratings' rating categories is \par contained in "S&P Global Ratings Definitions" at \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352 }}}\uc2 \f2  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par www.capitaliq.com. All ratings affected by this rating action can be found on \par S&P Global Ratings' public website at www.stan