\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Downgrades Bank Handlowy's VR to 'bbb+'/RWN; Affirms IDR at 'A-'\b0\par\par\uc2 3,026 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -Warsaw-14 September 2022: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has downgraded \uc2 Bank Handlowy w Warszawie S.A\uc2 .'s (Handlowy) Viability Rating (VR) to 'bbb+' from 'a-' and maintained it on Rating Watch Negative (RWN). \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also affirmed the bank's Long-Term Issuer Default Ratings (IDR) at 'A-' with a Stable Outlook.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade of the bank's VR reflects our opinion that the operating environment presents increased risks to the bank' standalone credit profile. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  downgraded the operating environment score for Polish banks to 'bbb' from 'bbb+' (see also '\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Places 5 Polish Banks' VRs and 3 IDRs on RWN' published on 5 August 2022 on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 ), reflecting the increased willingness of the Polish authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector and impose large additional costs on banks, while the macroeconomic outlook has also worsened. Consequently, we believe risks to the bank's asset quality and capitalisation have risen, although most financial metrics currently remain solid and profitability has strengthened. We recognise the bank's business model is more resilient than other rated Polish peers but it cannot be insulated from the operating environment risks.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has withdrawn Handlowy's Support Rating of '1' as it is no longer relevant to the agency's coverage following the publication of its updated Bank Rating Criteria on 12 November 2021. In line with the updated criteria, we have assigned Handlowy a Shareholder Support Rating (SSR) of 'a-'.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Handlowy's IDRs and SSR reflect a high probability of support from the bank's majority owner, \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . (A/Stable). The Stable Outlook on the Long-Term IDR reflects that on the parent's rating. The bank's Short-Term IDR of 'F1' is the higher of two possibilities corresponding to a Long-Term IDR of 'A-'. This reflects our opinion that the parent's propensity to support is typically more certain in the near term. The National Ratings are at the higher end of the scale given strong potential support from the parent.\par \par \uc2 Handlowy's VR is the highest among Polish banks. It balances strong capital and liquidity buffers, conservative risk controls and a low-risk business model against the bank's moderate franchise and \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an inherently riskier and more volatile emerging market. We also take into consideration the bank's low credit losses through the cycle and the strategic focus on lending to high quality corporate and retail borrowers.\par \par \uc2 The RWN on the VR reflects limited transparency over the scope and timeframe of the exit from the retail business and uncertainty over its impact on the bank's risk and funding profile. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects that resolution of the RWN may take longer than six months, outside of the normal Rating Watch horizon.\par \par \uc2 Shareholder Support: We believe that \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  would have a strong propensity to support Handlowy, if needed, given the importance of the corporate banking business in the region for \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and possible reputational damage, should its \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 -based subsidiary default.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Risk in Operating Environment: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  recently revised down the Polish banks' operating environment score to 'bbb' from 'bbb+' because it views the recently introduced payment holidays on local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgages as further evidence of the willingness of the Polish authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector and impose large additional costs on banks. This most recent measure follows a sizeable bank tax and the substantial provisions banks make for legal risks relating to Swiss franc mortgage loans.\par \par \uc2 Benign Asset Quality; Increased Macro Risks: Macroeconomic volatility, in particular higher interest rates and inflation, will weigh on the Stage 3 loans ratio (end-1H22: 3.5%) over the coming quarters. However, asset quality risks are mitigated by the bank's small loan book (32% of total assets at end-1H22), with broader asset quality being more closely aligned with sovereign creditworthiness due to large holdings of Polish sovereign and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  securities.\par \par \uc2 Rate Rises Boost Earnings: Handlowy's operating profit spiked in 1H22 to 7.1% of risk-weighted assets (RWA; 3.5% in 2021) as its interest margin strongly benefited from the reconstructed securities portfolio and the bank reported low cost of risk. We expect operating profitability to remain solid in 2022. However, it will be pressured by weaker loan growth and investments prospects, increased operating expenses and rising impairment charges, but also costs related to government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We assume deposit cost will increase gradually over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Solid Capital Buffers: Handlowy's dividend pay-out coupled with the negative revaluation of the securities portfolio due to rising market interest rates reduced the bank's elevated common equity Tier 1 ratio to 17.7% at end-1H22. We believe capitalisation will remain comfortable in the long term, although it is currently unclear how the exit from retail will impact capital.\par \par \uc2 Strong Funding and Liquidity: Handlowy's funding and liquidity position is a rating strength. Its stable customer deposit base (representing about 90% of liabilities at end-1H22) reflects a sound domestic franchise among a targeted group of clients and well-established customer relationships. The gross loans/deposits ratio remains low at about 50%. Liquidity buffers are very strong with the liquidity coverage ratio at 155% at end-1H22.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The Long-Term IDR will be downgraded if our view of parental support weakens. A support revision could be triggered by a downgrade of \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's Long-Term IDR or a reassessment of the propensity of the parent to provide support, for example if the group decided to sell the subsidiary or if its strategic importance is diminished.\par \par \uc2 The VR will be downgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes that the sale of the retail part of the business has a material negative effect on Handlowy's financial profile, and that ultimately the bank's standalone assessment is no longer above peers and above the operating environment score.\par \par \uc2 Due to its high level in the local context, the VR has narrow headroom to absorb a weakening in the credit profile. Therefore, Handlowy's VR could also be downgraded if the negative impact of the weakening economic environment results in deterioration of key financial metrics materially beyond our expectations or if further government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  lead to high additional costs for the sector, including Handlowy.\par \par \uc2 The bank's National Ratings are sensitive to changes to the Long-Term IDR.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's Long-Term IDR could be upgraded if \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's Long-Term IDR was upgraded or if there is a supportive revision of the resolution strategy of the group with respect to Handlowy.\par \par \uc2 An affirmation of the VR and removal from RWN could result from our perception of only modest narrowing of the bank's franchise after the exit of the retail business and if we conclude that the bank's risk and financial profile is still commensurate with a 'bbb+' assessment, or if the transaction is cancelled.\par \par \uc2 Given the level of the VR and the RWN on the rating, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  does not foresee an upgrade in the immediate term. A prerequisite would be the reassessment of the operating environment score to at least 'bbb+'.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The Business Profile score of 'bbb' is above the 'bb' category implied score because of the following adjustment reasons: Business Model (positive).\par \par \uc2 The Funding & Liquidity score of 'a-' is above the 'bbb' category implied score because of the following adjustment reasons: Liquidity Coverage (positive).\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 Handlowy's IDRs and SSR are linked to \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's IDRs.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Handlowy has an ESG Relevance Score for Management Strategy of '4'. This reflects our view of elevated government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the Polish banking sector, which impacts the banks' operating environment and their ability to define and execute on their strategies and has a negative implication for their ratings in combination with other factors.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entities, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entities. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Bank Handlowy w Warszawie S.A\uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; A-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; F1\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA+(pol); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; National Short Term Rating; Affirmed; F1+(pol)\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Downgrade; bbb+; Rating Watch Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Shareholder Support Rating; New Rating; a-\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Withdrawn; WD\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Artur Szeski,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +48 22 103 3015\par \par \uc2 artur.szeski@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  Irelan