\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-War in Ukraine puts pressure on East European banks to prop up sinking  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 17:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers, no changes)\par \par \uc2 By Gergely Szakacs and Alan Charlish\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST/WARSAW, March 4 (Reuters) - Russia's invasion of Ukraine is putting pressure on central banks along the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's eastern flank to prop up their weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , forcing Czech and Polish rate-setters into market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hungary into prolonged rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The market sell-off in the wake of the Russian invasion on Feb. 24 and a looming energy price shock in Europe due to a surge in global oil and gas prices compound already strong underlying price pressures in the region, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' sharp weakening could fuel additional inflation.\par \par \uc2 Economists say against this backdrop, the region's central banks - which have been fighting inflation with rate hikes since June 2021 - have little choice now but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed and tighten policy further even as the growth outlook is set to deteriorate as a result of the military conflict.\par \par \uc2 "The implications of the conflict for CEE-4 monetary policy are firmly hawkish, in our view," economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "While the crisis is likely to have a dampening effect on growth, the combination of higher commodity prices and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation is significantly inflationary."\par \par \uc2 "Moreover, this pro-inflationary shock is occurring at a time when CEE-4 central banks have been trying to tighten financial conditions to bring exceptionally high inflation rates under control. The recent depreciation of CEE-4 exchange rates is making this task more difficult."\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has raised its forecast for peak official rates by 50 bps to 5.5% in Poland, Hungary, the Czech Republic and Romania.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \uc2 Czech National Bank (CNB) \uc2 Czech National Bank\uc2  (CNB) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market against excessive volatility and crown depreciation, its first such move since abandoning a cap on the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2017, lifting the unit from 10-month-lows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CNB\uc2 , which said "it is active on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and is conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mitigate excessive fluctuations and depreciation of the crown", followed the \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already raised rates by 425 basis points since last June to bring its rate to a 20-year high of 4.5% and analysts see chances the bank will raise rates by up to 50 basis points at its next meeting in late March.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of Poland\uc2  also stepped in to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for zlotys, while the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  delivered its biggest rate increase since late-2008 to rein in the forint from successive all-time-lows.\par \par \uc2 Despite those moves, all three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which started the year on a strong footing, have remained in the red for the year, with the zloty trading at 13-year-lows. The forint plumbed record lows this week, falling to 385.97 versus the euro by Friday afternoon from 372 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 DOUBLE-DIGIT INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Central banks cannot do anything other than remain hawkish in the near term, economists at Wood and Co. said in a note, projecting a 2 percentage point drop in 2022 economic growth compared to their previous baseline due to the inflation shock.\par \par \uc2 "There is a high risk of double-digit inflation this year, moderating, but staying two-to-three times the central banks' targets in 2023E," they said.\par \par \uc2 Economists say rising Romanian swap yields also signal \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the leu stable.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has denied comment but it has a long track record of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets next Tuesday when it is expected to raise its key rate by 50 bps to 3.25%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  holds a non-rate setting meeting on Tuesday, which some economists expect to turn into a rate-setting meeting after the bank exhausted its room for manoeuvre with Thursday's massive hike.\par \par \uc2 The bank raised its one-week deposit rate to 5.35%, just 5 bps shy of the top of its interest rate corridor.\par \par \uc2 Economists at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  said the NBH could also end up having to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year if government price caps to keep a lid on inflation, which rose to a near 15-year-high in January, are removed after an April general election.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that rate hikes cannot strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on their own. If EUR/HUF remains close to 380 for longer, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  pass-through could exceed 1pp," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  said. The NBH has declined comment on direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jason Hovet in PRAGUE and Luiza Ilie in BUCHAREST Editing by Elaine Hardcastle) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-4T17:01:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220304ei3403att\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
