\par\par \b \uc2 Polish Zloty Could Fall on Profit-Taking After Rate Decision -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2021\par 11:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0930 GMT - The Polish zloty could weaken if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves its policy measures on hold in a decision later Wednesday as it could prompt investors to take profits following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent appreciation, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. "The PLN has significantly appreciated (over 4%) against the EUR since reaching lows of around 4.66 in March, breaching the 4.50 area, around which the \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past to fight excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  analysts say. "A steady NBP may thus trigger some profit taking as a knee-jerk reaction, but hawkish language by the bank may still spur buying-on-dips interest once again." EUR/PLN falls 0.2% to 4.4541. The policy decision is expected at 1100 GMT. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  's policy decision later Wednesday won't have a major impact on the Canadian dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to taper asset purchases further, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "In particular as it is likely to be unhappy about the appreciation of the Canadian dollar to almost 1.20 in USD/CAD and will probably want to avoid fuelling this development further by sending out a restrictive signal today," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's \uc2 Antje Praefcke\uc2  says. The labor market hasn't recovered from coronavirus and accelerating inflation is expected to be temporary, she says. In April the \uc2 BOC\uc2  reduced weekly asset purchases and accelerated the timetable for a possible interest rate rise. USD/CAD drops 0.2% to 1.2086. The \uc2 BOC\uc2  's decision is due at 1400 GMT. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT - The \uc2 U.K. Debt Management Office\uc2  's planned sale of GBP1 billion in the 0.125% August 2031 index-linked gilt should be well-absorbed by the market, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  . Whilst 10-year breakevens--the difference between the 10-year yield on a nominal bond and the real 10-year yield on an inflation-linked bond-- "are at extreme rich levels outright, the sector has cheapened-up on the curve and the bond has also cheapened versus its close neighbours on micro-relative value [metrics]," analysts at the bank say. Additionally, the relatively small size of supply should mean the bond is "taken down fine," they say. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT - Norwegian mainland gross domestic product recovered slightly in April, growing 0.3% on the month, above the \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  's and the consensus forecast of 0.2%, says \uc2 SEB\uc2 . Private consumption continued to decline, driven by weak services, while goods consumption recovered from a weak 1Q. The GDP outcome in April will have very limited impact on next week's \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  rate decision, it says. "We expect the bank to note that growth seems to have stabilized during April but to put much more focus on the forward-looking factors." Yesterday's strong Regional Network Report combined with the latest improvement in the labor market suggest that economic activity will pick up in coming months and that the recovery may be even faster than \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  assumed in March, \uc2 SEB\uc2  says. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT - Inflation-linked bonds have been among the best-performing segments of the bond market so far this year, says Soenke Siemssen, head of client portfolio management at Nomura Asset Management Europe. With a positive performance of 0.8%, they have clearly outperformed Bunds and U.S. Treasuries, which have lost 3.2% and 3.5%, respectively, in the same period, he says. The gap is even more pronounced when looked at a three-year horizon, according to Nomura AM. Globally, linkers gained 14.2% in euro terms between May 31, 2018 and May 31 this year, while global bonds, including investment-grade corporate bonds, gained 8.5%, despite central banks' support, he says. Siemssen says market participants' significantly higher expectations are behind this development. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT - The fully adjusted trade balance in Germany rebounded in April, but less than expected, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. Germany's trade surplus totaled EUR15.8 billion after EUR14.0 billion in March. "The numbers now suggest that the seasonally adjusted surplus in Germany is settling at a lower trend, but it is too soon to say for sure," Pantheon's economists say. Exports rose by 0.3%, while imports fell by 1.7% month-on-month. Pantheon forecasts imports have further to fall in the near term, so that the headline surplus will edge higher in the next few months, probably to EUR17 billion to EUR18 billion. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0815 GMT - Sterling rises after \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Andy Haldane\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to consider withdrawing stimulus to contain rising U.K. inflation. Haldane, who is leaving the BOE after the June 24 meeting, said in an interview with LBC radio that inflation could overshoot the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target for longer than planned and it was important that the U.K. didn't become too dependent on "monetary medicine." He said "at some stage, we need to start turning off the tap when it comes to the monetary policy support we've been providing." GBP/USD rises 0.2% to 1.4186 and EUR/GBP falls 0.1% to 0.8591. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT - U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's American Jobs Plan will likely provide the biggest boost to manufacturing and construction, economists at Oxford Economics say, and forecast the plan will raise output in these sectors by around 2.3% by the end of 2023. In the services sector, professional and business services stand to gain the most as the plan's push to upgrade transportation and communication networks, increase R&D and green investment, and bolster U.S. manufacturing would generate demand for engineers, architects, consultants, and scientists, they say. Among the largest state economies, California, Georgia, Michigan, and North Carolina are likely to benefit the most from the plan, experiencing a one-percentage-point increase in output growth in 2022 and 2023, Oxford Economics says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0718 GMT - The EU is becoming more "sovereign like" in some of its debt management policies which is likely to further establish it as a high quality, regular issuer of liquid bonds, says Peter Allen Goves, fixed-income research analyst at \uc2 MFS Investment Management\uc2 . The EU will kick off syndicated bond deals under the NextGenerationEU program this month, possibly as early as next week. The NGEU issuance will give a rising importance to green bond supply given that up to 30% of NGEU could be in green bond format, he says. "This is likely to be an important boost to the sector," he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT - The euro will likely continue trading in a narrow range against the dollar before the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's June 16 policy meeting, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "It is unlikely that anyone wants to take too firmly a position ahead of the Fed meeting next week as it is uncertain what view Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  will take on the unfavorable data situation (significant rise in inflation rates, recently disappointing labor market report)," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Antje Praefcke\uc2  says. That suggests EUR/USD will stay range bound for now but in coming weeks it could become clear that the exchange rate has seen its peak at just shy of 1.23, she says. EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 1.2183. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to broadly maintain the current asset purchase pace in 3Q, meaning EUR80 billion a month under the Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme and EUR20 billion a month under the regular Asset Purchase Programme, Konstantin Veit, portfolio manager at Pimco, says. Though, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  might decide to signal a somewhat lower PEPP purchase pace due to seasonality considerations, he says. "But we don't expect the message to be one of reduced flow support," Veit says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields are little changed ahead of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's monetary policy meeting on Thursday. Market expectations tilt towards the \uc2 ECB\uc2  maintaining pandemic emergency bond purchases in the third quarter at a broadly similar level to that in the second quarter, prompting investors to price out a near-term tapering. Significant supply is thus also less of a concern, analysts say. "Outright markets are brushing off the renewed duration supply," says Michael Leister, head of interest rates strategy at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . Besides German and Portuguese bond auctions, Greece is expected to tap a 10-year bond via syndication. The 10-year Bund yield is trading 0.2 basis points lower at -0.221%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 09, 2021 05:30 ET (09:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020210609eh69000ya\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
