\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish c.bank sees higher inflation and GDP growth in 2021\b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 16:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, March 3 (Reuters) - Inflation and gross domestic product (GDP) growth in Poland should be higher in 2021 than previously expected, \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday, after it left its benchmark interest rate unchanged at a record low of 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Emerging Europe's largest economy proved relatively resilient to the coronavirus pandemic in 2020, contracting by 2.8%, and economists expect vaccinations in 2021 to aid its recovery.\par \par \uc2 "Following the fall in GDP in 2020, a rise in economic activity is expected in 2021, although the scale and pace of the recovery are uncertain," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "The further course of the pandemic and its impact on the economic situation in Poland and abroad continue to be the main source of uncertainty."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it saw GDP growth at 2.6\u8211\'20\'135.3% in 2021, compared to 0.8\u8211\'20\'134.5% in its November 2020 projection. In 2022, it expects growth of 4.0\u8211\'20\'136.9%, compared to 3.8\u8211\'20\'137.8% in the November projection.\par \par \uc2 It sees CPI of 2.7\u8211\'20\'133.6% in 2021, compared to 1.8\u8211\'20\'133.2% in the November projection. In 2022, it sees CPI of 2.0\u8211\'20\'133.6%, compared to 1.6\u8211\'20\'133.6% in the November projection.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would continue to buy government and government-backed bonds on the secondary market. It said it would also continue to offer bill discount credit, which makes bank loans cheaper.\par \par \uc2 It reiterated that if the zloty is too strong it could damage Poland's economic recovery and said again that it was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stop this.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rate has been at a record low of 0.1% since rate-setters slashed the cost of borrowing three times by a cumulative 140 basis points during the first wave of the economically damaging COVID-19 pandemic in the spring of 2020.\par \par \uc2 All but one of 18 analysts polled by Reuters had expected rates to remain on hold on Wednesday. (Reporting by Alan Charlish, Pawel Florkiewicz and Anna Koper; Editing by Bernadette Baum) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-3T16:54:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210303eh33036nv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
