\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Polish  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  keeps rates steady, says  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2  possible\b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2021\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details from statement)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Feb 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to keep its key interest rate on hold at 0.1% on Wednesday, as expected, and reiterated that it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the zloty amid speculation of a further cut in borrowing costs fades.\par \par \uc2 While financial markets have been pricing in some chance of a rate cut in the coming months, recent statements from central bankers and data showing the economy's resilience to the COVID-19 pandemic have led most economists to conclude that stable rates are the most likely scenario until the end of 2022.\par \par \uc2 "It can be expected that...economic activity will increase in 2021, although the scale of the recovery will mainly depend on the epidemiological situation and the related restrictions," the bank said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 All 18 analysts polled by Reuters had expected rates to remain on hold on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The decision to keep interest rates unchanged is not surprising," said Mateusz Sutowicz, a financial market analyst at Bank Millennium.\par \par \uc2 "According to recent comments from the governor of the National Bank..., the March meeting, during which we will learn about the assumptions of the latest macroeconomic projection, may prove decisive for the outlook for interest rates."\par \par \uc2 Poland's benchmark interest rate has been at a record low of 0.1% since rate-setters slashed the cost of borrowing three times by a cumulative 140 basis points during the first wave of the economically damaging COVID-19 pandemic in the spring of 2020.\par \par \uc2 The bank said it would continue to purchase government and government-secured bonds and to offer bill discount credit, which makes bank loans cheaper.\par \par \uc2 The zloty, which on Tuesday firmed past the 4.50 mark against the euro, a level many economists see as the one that could trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , firmed slightly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement and was trading at 4.4835 at 1558 GMT, 0.06% softer on the day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said that the strength of the zloty poses a threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery, and in December it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Alan Charlish, Pawel Florkiewicz, Anna Wlodarczak-Semczuk and Agnieszka Barteczko; Editing by Steve Orlofsky and Mark Heinrich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-2-3T17:12:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210203eh23031ki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
