\par\par \b \uc2 Polish  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  's  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Interventions \highlight\uc2  Could Prompt  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2021\par 17:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1117 ET - The strategy of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market could lead to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war with peers in the Central and Eastern European region, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "While the NBP [\uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  ] favours a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prolonged \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb zloty's gains may have negative consequences," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Piotr Matys\uc2  says. Poland could be labelled a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator and its strategy could result in a "full-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" in the CEE region, he says. The NBP said on Wednesday that it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a stronger zloty risks reducing the pace of the economic recovery. EUR/PLN rises 0.1% to 4.5448 after earlier reaching a one-week high of 4.5528, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1103 ET - The rise in U.S. jobless claims highlights some further downside risk to labor markets from economic restrictions associated with elevated Covid-19 cases, but data is obscured by large seasonal adjustments, \uc2 Citi\uc2 's economist \uc2 Andrew Hollenhorst\uc2  says. "We would be very cautious in reading new labor market weakness into this week's report," he says, noting that it is typical for jobless claims to spike in January with the conclusion of the holiday period and the reading may include catch-up from filing that didn't occur over the prior weeks. "Neither Fed nor market outlooks will change substantially as some further near-term weakness is expected, followed by a stronger expansion as vaccines become more broadly distributed," Hollenhorst says.(xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1050 ET - There will be no improvement in the U.S. jobs market until containment measures are eased, and that won't happen soon given the slow rate of vaccination, says \uc2 James Knightley\uc2  , ING's chief international economist. The jump in initial jobless claims to 965,000 in the week of Jan. 9 shows the strains that Covid-19 continues to exert on the economy and the jobs market, he says, expecting another fall in employment in January. "These sorts of economic numbers suggest better management and more money is required to accelerate the vaccination program," Knightley says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)A bumper supply of European senior bank debt is contributing to widening spreads, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Cem Keltek. Bail-in senior debt of EUR8.3 billion makes up almost a quarter of this year's euro-denominated investment-grade benchmark issuance. "Italian Bank Debt Underperforms Due to Hefty Supply, Politics -- Market Talk," at 0941 GMT, incorrectly said the issuance related to specifically Italian bank debt in the headline and throughout the article. It also misspelled the analyst's name as Cem Kelpek. The correct version follows: Bumper supply of European senior bank debt is one of the factors driving spreads wider, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . "Beyond recent jitters in Italian names from domestic political uncertainty and still lingering loan book concerns on the back of persisting lockdowns, supply seems to be at least partially to blame," says credit strategist Cem Keltek. After issuance of EUR8.3 billion in bail-in senior debt, it makes up almost a quarter of this year's EUR34 billion investment-grade benchmark issuance, significantly above its index share of around 13.5% by market value, he says. "Near term the combination of more supply, growing lockdown fallout and political uncertainty in Italy could see banks' underperformance extend," Keltek says, adding that the bank nonetheless maintains its small overweight view in financials and continues to see dips as better buying opportunities. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0849 ET - US import prices rose 0.9% from a month earlier in December, driven by rising petroleum costs. Petroleum jumped 9.1% from the prior month but even with recent gains, import fuel prices were down 19.5% in 2020, the largest calendar-year drop since 2015. Outside of petroleum, import prices rose 0.4% from the prior month. As with other inflation measures, import prices have gyrated through the pandemic along with fluctuating demand and supply chain disruptions. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 ET - An improved global economic outlook should lift the risk-sensitive Australian dollar but gains are likely to be limited due to the country's accommodative monetary policy and trade tensions with China, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. China's economic recovery and the prospect of greater U.S. fiscal stimulus will feed the reflation trade, boding well for AUD, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  says. The reflation trade means demand for assets exposed to economic growth. "That said, the [\uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  's] quantitative easing policy and trade tensions between Australia and China may keep a lid on upside potential," Foley says. \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  raises its 12-month forecast for AUD/USD to 0.78 from 0.77 but expects it to trade sideways in coming months. AUD/USD rises 0.4% to 0.7760. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0832 ET - The euro could fall as long positions--or bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising--remain stretched amid Italian political uncertainty and rising eurozone coronavirus cases, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. "Italian political unrest and a sense that Europe is behind other major economies in vaccine deployment could, in the short term, keep up pressure for EUR/USD to break below 1.2120 and slip back to 1.20," SocGen \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kit Juckes says. Italian Prime Minister \uc2 Giuseppe Conte\uc2  's future was put in doubt on Wednesday after former Prime Minister \uc2 Matteo Renzi\uc2  withdrew his Italia \uc2 Viva\uc2  party from the country's ruling coalition in a dispute over the government's recovery plan. EUR/USD trades down 0.3% at 1.2116. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 ET - The strengthening euro is likely to squeeze luxury-goods companies' earnings this year and next, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. Hedging should help push \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effects partly into the second half of 2021 and above all next year, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. These effects take the form of a potential 8% squeeze on luxury companies' EBIT, the bank says. However, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  thinks the sector's bigger players have elbow room to mitigate this through price increases outside the eurozone without dragging volumes--indeed, Jefferies noted last week that \uc2 LVMH\uc2  's powerhouse Louis Vuitton had already bumped its prices at the start of the year. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  singles out \uc2 Prada\uc2  as likely to be among the players hit hardest by the stronger euro, given the Italian fashion house's already tight margins.(joshua.kirby@dowjones.com; @joshualeokirby)\par \par \uc2 0700 ET - The Hungarian forint rises against the euro after official data showed headline inflation remained steady but core inflation inched higher in December. Headline inflation stayed at an annual rate of 2.7% while core inflation, which strips out volatile items like food and fuel prices, rose to 4.0% from 3.9% in November. The \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  targets 3% inflation with a tolerance band of a percentage point either side. With core inflation at the top of bank's tolerance band, its unlikely to cut interest rates and will maintain a "cautious, wait-and-see" policy stance, ING economist \uc2 Peter Virovacz\uc2  says. EUR/HUF falls to 359.1847 from 360.7000 before the data and is down 0.3% on the day.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 ET - Italian government bonds are expected to be more volatile until the path to solving the political crisis becomes clearer, but analysts remain constructive at this stage, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  . The spread between the 10-year Italian BTP and German Bund, which has been at historically tight levels, could widen towards 200 basis points in case of early elections, according to the bank. This scenario is seen as unlikely as the government could still find support from smaller parties or independents in order to carry on. "We remain constructive on Italian government bonds, which continue to offer a decent return potential compared with those of other peripheral Eurozone countries, and are supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 ET - The Norwegian krone should strengthen in coming quarters on higher oil prices, improved risk appetite and the prospect of an interest rate rise, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. Oil prices have risen in recent weeks while markets are "generally optimistic" as the economic outlook has brightened, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's \uc2 Antje Praefcke\uc2  says. The \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  in December signalled it could raise its key rate by the second half of 2022, which would make it the first G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do so and points towards rising inflation-adjusted rates, she says. NOK should be "the silent outperformer" over coming quarters even if there are temporary setbacks and corrections, she says. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects EUR/NOK to fall to 10.20 by December, from 10.3136 currently. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 ET - Canada's Alimentation \uc2 Couche-Tard\uc2  's potential takeover of Carrefour would harm the overall credit profile of France's biggest supermarket chain after the tie-up, ING says. Carrefour received a nonbinding offer of EUR20.00 a share from \uc2 Couche-Tard\uc2  , which would be financed with cash, yet "since Carrefour and \uc2 Couche-Tard\uc2  have similar credit profiles, we foresee this will have a deteriorating effect on overall credit metrics," the Dutch bank says. According to French media reports, French ministers have announced opposition to the takeover plan. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 14, 2021 11:17 ET (16:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020210114eh1e002m4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
