\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish c.bank ready to cut rates if there is a third wave of coronavirus\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2021\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Jan 5 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since mid-December to weaken the zloty and could cut interest rates this quarter if there is a third wave of the pandemic, governor \uc2 Adam Glapinski\uc2  said in an interview published on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The interview with financial news website Obserwator Finansowy repeated extracts of the interview first published on Dec. 30 when Glapinski pointed to possible rate cuts, after previously saying that rates would remain at a record low of 0.1% for a long time.\par \par \uc2 "In the event of a third wave of the pandemic in the winter months, it would be possible to further lower interest rates in the first quarter of 2021," Glapinski said in the full version of the interview.\par \par \uc2 However, Glapinski also said that in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's baseline scenario of a stabilising pandemic and gradually improving economic situation, there should be no need to change the monetary policy in the coming quarters.\par \par \uc2 While some parts of Europe are now seeing another surge in COVID-19 cases, daily cases in Poland are not as high as seen at the beginning of November during a second wave of the virus.\par \par \uc2 The country has seen 1,330,543 cases of COVID-19 and 29,502 deaths.\par \par \uc2 Glapinksi confirmed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the zloty and support the economy.\par \par \uc2 "The NBP (\uc2 National Bank of Poland\uc2 ) Management Board has been purchasing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since mid-December 2020 in order to strengthen the impact of loosening the NBP monetary policy on the economy," he was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 On Dec. 18, a source close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the zloty.\par \par \uc2 While Glapinski said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's baseline scenario assumes a gradual economic recovery and inflation in line with its target, he added it was difficult to predict what will happen.\par \par \uc2 "...It may take us longer than we anticipate to return the economy to normal," he said\par \par \uc2 "This, in turn, would also create a risk of a drop in price growth below the inflation target, which in the current macroeconomic situation would be additionally unfavourable for the economy."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last cut interest rates in May, targets inflation of 2.5% plus or minus one percentage point. (Reporting by Alan Charlish, Agnieszka Barteczko and Joanna Plucinska; Editing by Susan Fenton) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-1-5T14:22:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210105eh1501wr1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
