\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS-Closing door on volatile 2020, Emerging Europe eyes rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2020\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Marton Dunai and Alan Charlish\par     BUDAPEST/WARSAW, Dec 31 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint ended\par 2020 down nearly 10% against the euro, losing more than any\par other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in emerging Europe even as the Polish zloty\par weakened considerably in the last trading sessions amid talk of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     As everywhere, the COVID-19 pandemic set the tone throughout\par most of the year, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first hammered in March, when\par the coronavirus reached the region and lockdown measures sent\par economies into a massive contraction.\par     Of the three most liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region, the Czech\par crown recovered the most of those losses, ending the year down\par about 3.4%, according to data at 0920 GMT.\par     The Polish zloty was down 7.5%, plunging at the end of the\par year as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on Dec. 18 to weaken the zloty and thereby\par support Polish exports.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened by as much as 1.77% on Tuesday,\par raising speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again. Reuters could not confirm that.\par     "It's very volatile, there is very low liquidity, probably\par the national bank is asking for prices," said a Warsaw-based\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader. "Today I haven't seen (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) ... or\par maybe there is some position squaring at the end of the year."\par     Political risks also weighed on the forint as Poland and\par Hungary threatened to veto the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 1.85 trillion\par euro ($2.27 trillion) budget and coronavirus rescue package over\par rule-of-law conditions on accessing those funds.\par     According to a Reuters poll of analysts, the Czech crown and\par the Polish zloty are expected to lead central European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher in the next year as the region recovers from\par the pandemic.\par     In the poll, conducted before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par were made public in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the zloty was expected to strengthen\par to as far as 4.37 against the euro, which would be a 5% gain\par from Thursday's levels. \par     Analysts expect the Czech crown to firm to 25.80 over the\par next year, or 1.9% from Thursday's level. The Hungarian forint\par is also expected to firm 1.9% from Thursday's level to 358.00.\par \par     "A strong growth recovery (3.5-4% in 2021-2022 across the\par region) with the tailwind of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  budget bodes well for CEE3\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in a note. "We expect an\par additional 3-5% appreciation of CEE3 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vs EUR in 2021."\par     "Equities are likely to benefit from reopening/recovery in\par Germany, combined with gradual monetary tightening domestically\par (we expect Czech Republic to hike first in 2H2021, followed by\par Poland and Hungary in 2022)."\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NBH) has said it would hold\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap tenders at the end of each quarter if\par needed to reduce market volatility.\par     On Tuesday and Wednesday, the NBH provided a combined 1.18\par billion euros ($1.45 billion) worth of liquidity to commercial\par banks.\par     \par     \par             CEE        SNAPSHOT   AT 1003 CET                    \par             MARKETS                                      \par                        \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                  \par                        S                                 \par                        Latest     Previous     Daily     Change\par                        bid        close        change    in 2020\par  Czech                   26.3150      26.2250    -0.34%    -3.36%\par  crown                                                   \par  Hungary                365.1000     365.0500    -0.01%    -9.30%\par  forint                                                  \par  Polish                   4.5950       4.5755    -0.42%    -7.37%\par  zloty                                                   \par  Romanian                 4.8685       4.8690    +0.01%    -1.65%\par  leu                                                     \par  Croatian                 7.5478       7.5483    +0.01%    -1.36%\par  kuna                                                    \par  Serbian                117.5400     117.5600    +0.02%    +0.03%\par  dinar                                                   \par  Note:      calculated from                    1800 CET          \par  dail