\par\par \b \uc2 CEE  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War Potentially Emerging -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2020\par 18:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1212 ET - A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war in Central and Eastern Europe may be emerging after reports \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the zloty from extending recent gains versus the euro, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. It's "difficult to expect" that the Hungarian or Czech central banks will stand still and watch \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "acting on purpose" to weaken the zloty when a stronger forint and koruna "gradually erode" the competitiveness of Hungarian and Czech exporters, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Piotr Matys\uc2  says. "Potential structural changes in global supply chains mean that stakes are too high to allow your neighbor to gain competitive advantage through a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says, referring to the diversification of supply chains away from Asia due to coronavirus. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - The demand for N95 respirators needed to fight the coronavirus pandemic continues to far exceed the available supply from both US manufacturers and foreign imports, the \uc2 US International Trade Commission\uc2  says in a report. While domestic production has more than tripled since 2019, barriers, including a shortage of filtration material, a lengthy and costly approval process and investment hesitation, continue to stymie output in some cases, the Commission says. (austen.hufford@wsj.com; @austenhufford)\par \par \uc2 1107 ET - A decision by Canada's most populous province, Ontario, to move into a regionwide shutdown for 28 days to contain Covid-19 spread likely upends employment data for January and pushes Canadian GDP into negative territory for 1Q, economists say. Ontario's measures kick in Saturday, and means nonessential businesses will close their doors. Also, the return to school will be delayed, forcing parents to juggle work and childcare. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says GDP will now fall further below prepandemic levels, and 1Q "will probably now be negative." \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  anticipates upheaval with jobs data, although economists at the firm argue the effect on GDP will be less severe than this spring as some businesses have learned to adapt. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1104 ET - The approval of a Covid-19 stimulus package through Congress includes $13B apportioned for agriculture, a welcome fresh wave of government aid for the beleaguered industry. "The assistance provided by this bill will go a long way in providing the certainty corn growers need to recover from the impact of the coronavirus on our industry," says John Linder, president of the \uc2 National Corn Growers Association\uc2  . The bill is particularly beneficial for the ethanol industry, with biofuel producers deemed eligible for aid and tax credits extended. "More than half of the ethanol industry shut down during the extraordinary demand collapse in the spring," \uc2 Renewable Fuels Association\uc2  head \uc2 Geoff Cooper\uc2  says. (kirk.maltais@wsj.com; @kirkmaltais)\par \par \uc2 1055 ET - Sterling is likely to remain volatile until there is more clarity on whether a U.K.-EU trade deal will be reached before the Brexit transition period ends on December 31, \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  says. Trading spot sterling is "very futile" and investors should instead buy two-week or longer options that bet on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising versus the euro or the dollar, \uc2 Saxo Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 John Hardy\uc2  says. However, the ceiling for sterling gains on any trade deal could prove "rather low" once an initial rally is in the rear-view mirror, he says. "Sterling is beset with the same structural issues as the U.S. dollar (external deficit), with only its still somewhat cheap valuation working in its favor." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1050 ET - Peru's government has increased social restrictions over the holidays as it aims to reduce crowds to prevent the spread of Covid-19. Authorities reduced the number of people allowed in shopping to 40% of normal capacity amid large crowds before Christmas, while banning visits to busy beaches in Lima. The government also expands a curfew that will now begin at 11 p.m. Peru is seeing a rise in cases in some regions after recent months of cases declining. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1020 ET - The U.K. economy is likely to be "extremely depressed" in the first quarter of 2021 as a third wave of coronavirus infections develops, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. "We expect GDP to remain about 9% below its fourth quarter 2019 peak, replicating the [2020] fourth quarter's likely shortfall," Pantheon economist \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2  says. Persistently weak demand in the first quarter due to restrictions to contain rising coronavirus cases will have "long-term consequences" as it will lead to more business closures and higher unemployment, he says. Pantheon expects the unemployment rate to rise to 6.5% in the second quarter of 2021 from 4.9% in the three months to October. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 ET - The dollar is likely to come under sustained downward pressure in 2021 as the U.S. economy recovers from the coronavirus pandemic and drives investors away from safe-haven assets, ING says. "The outlook for 2021 has undoubtedly brightened with vaccine optimism," ING economist James Knightly says. Rising coronavirus cases and containment measures may mean "near-term pain" for the economy but "things look good" for the medium to long term, he says. An economic recovery may also push U.S. inflation higher in the second half of 2021 while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  keeps interest rates low, weighing on the dollar, he says. The dollar index is last up 0.4% at 90.3970. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 ET - The U.K. economy grew faster than previously estimated in the third quarter following a second-quarter slump, but the recovery was still relatively lackluster given looser coronavirus restrictions over the period, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. "That does not inspire a lot of confidence for the economy's ability to do better in the first quarter when we also have the disruption related to Brexit to contend with," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist \uc2 Elizabeth Martins\uc2  says. The data seem "ancient history" with a resurgence in coronavirus cases and stricter measures likely to interrupt the recovery, she says. GDP grew 16.0% from July to September, up from a previous estimate of 15.5% and compared to an 18.8% fall in the previous three months, according to official data released Tuesday. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 ET - The $900 billion fiscal relief package agreed by Congress won't represent as big a fiscal expansion as the headline figures suggest as it appears to mostly involve recycling the unused funding from previous stimulus deals this year, \uc2 Andrew Hunter\uc2  , senior U.S. economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , says. The package is unlikely to prevent the continued rapid spread of the coronavirus from weighing on the economy over the next few months, he says, as households are unlikely tospend the extra income with the virus still spreading rapidly and states imposing more severe restrictions. However, the agreement provides another reason to expect GDP growth will rebound markedly next year as vaccines are rolled out. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 ET - The \uc2 European Investment Bank\uc2  , the bloc's "climate bank" under the European Green Deal, needs to be more transparent about how it makes investment decisions due to climate change, say environmental lawyers who lodged a complaint with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's ombudsman. The lawyers--from CEE Bankwatch Network, Counter Balance and \uc2 ClientEarth\uc2  --say the \uc2 EIB\uc2  should publicly share more documents that outline which projects it chooses to finance and why, which projects are funded through intermediaries like commercial banks and how projects are monitored after they are invested in. This week, the \uc2 EIB\uc2  opened a consultation on its transparency policy. (dieter.holger@wsj.com; @dieterholger)\par \par \uc2 0859 ET - Environmental, social and governance concerns moved up on the agenda as responsible investment funds in the U.K. took in GBP7.8 billion between January to October in 2020, nearly half of all net money that went into funds overall, says the Investment Association, a trade group. The IA says that is four times higher than same period in 2019, with October earning the highest month in record after responsible funds drew more than GBP1 billion in that month. "Responsible investment came of age in 2020," the IA says. (dieter.holger@wsj.com; @dieterholger)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 22, 2020 12:12 ET (17:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020201222egcm00299\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
