\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Stocks plunge, zloty near 4-1/2 year low on British vote to leave \uc2 EU\b0\par\par\uc2 907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 09:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds stock markets, Polish tender, analyst on growth)\par     By Krisztina  Than\par     BUDAPEST, June 24 (Reuters) - Central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par fell and stock markets plunged on Friday after Britain voted to\par leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , with the Polish zloty tumbling to a\par 4-1/2 year low against the euro and some stock markets to\par multi-year lows.\par     Direct trade linkages with Britain account for 3-6 percent\par of the foreign trade of central eastern European countries, with\par the euro zone having a much bigger role. \par     But Britain is still Poland's second-biggest export market,\par with its exports to Britain in 2015 at around 12 billion euros.\par     Investors in Central Europe have been worried that the\par impact of Britain's exit could be bigger if it leads later to\par cuts in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  budget.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  development funds play a big role in boosting economic\par growth in poorer \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  states, so a reduction would be painful.\par There are also hundreds of thousands of east Europeans working\par in Britain.\par     "The big question is what happens to those who work there\par (in the UK)," said Janos Samu, an analyst at Concorde Securities\par in Budapest. "What hits us badly now is all this uncertainty."\par     "We see overall growth impact as biggest in (the) Czech\par (Republic) and then Hungary at around 0.5pp shaved off of growth\par under Brexit, Poland around 0.25pp and other countries minimal,"\par Peter Attard Montalto at Nomura said in a note.\par     The zloty fell to 4.52 to the euro in early trade\par but recouped some of its losses by 0711 GMT, to trade at 4.456.\par The forint fell 1.7 percent to 320, but firmed back\par to 318.20 later in highly volatile trading.\par     Warsaw's WIG20 bluechip index touched a 7-year low,\par but later recouped some losses. The broader market WIG \par fell to a 5-month low. Prague stocks fell to a 7-year low. \par     Shares in Poland's biggest bank, the state-controlled \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKO BP\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  fell more than 10 percent in early trade, becoming the\par biggest drag on the WIG20 index. \par     The Bucharest stock exchange's blue chip index fell\par 4.5 percent to a four-month low. Budapest stocks were\par down 4.6 percent at a three-and-half-month low.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  has called off a switch treasury\par debt tender initially planned for Monday due to market\par volatility.\par     "I don't think the zloty could weaken further than 4.60 per\par euro," said Piotr Poplawski, senior economist at ING BSK in\par Warsaw.\par     "Due to our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -loan problem we're very dependent on the SNB.\par The zloty has regained some ground just after they \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and as long as they keep defending the franc the zloty should be\par relatively stable," he added.\par     He added he did not see a chance of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near term. \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's deputy finance minister said the weakening of the\par zloty was not a risk to the Polish economy. He said he saw no\par reason to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which left interest rates on\par hold at a record-low 0.9 percent on Tuesday, said it was\par closely monitoring the British vote and related market\par developments and had all the necessary tools to continuously\par ensure financial stability. \par     The Czech crown, which is considered as a safe\par haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region, was relatively steady, easing only\par about 0.2 percent.\par                CEE MARKETS  SNAPSHOT   AT  0911 CET            \par                             \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          \par                             Latest    Previous   Daily   Change\par                             bid       close      change  in\par                                                          2016\par  Czech crown                 27.1210    27.0780  -0.16%  -0.45%\par  Hungary                    318.1900   314.6200  -1.12%  -1.12%\par  forint                                                  \par  Polish zloty                 4.4560     4.3675  -1.99%  -4.44%\par  Romanian leu                 4.5475     4.5245  -0.51%  -0.63%\par  Croatian                     7.5340     7.5252  -0.12%   1.39%\par  kuna                                                    \par  Serbian                    123.8300   123.9600   +0.10  -1.91%\par  dinar                                                %  \par  Note: daily   calculated   previous  close at   1800          \par  change        from                              CET     \par                             STOCKS                             \par                             Latest    Previous   Daily   Change\par                                       close      change  in\par                                                          2016\par  Prague                       772.96     855.26  -9.62%  