\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS-Poland leads losses amid nerves over British vote\b0\par\par\uc2 918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2016\par 15:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Prague stocks at 7-year low, Hungarian at 3-month low\par     * Zloty sets 3-week low vs euro\par     * Polish bond yields near highest since late Jan\par     * Pressure might ease if Fed comments are dovish\par \par  (Recasts, adding new comments, volatilities, CDSs)\par     By Sandor Peto and Bartosz Lada\par     BUDAPEST/WARSAW, June 14 (Reuters) - Poland led further\par declines in Central European assets on Tuesday due to worries of\par slower economic growth if Britain decides to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European\par Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a June 23 referendum.\par     "Investors are getting really nervous (over the British\par vote)," said Marcin Turkiewicz, head of mBank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers\par in Warsaw.\par     Regional equities and government bonds eased in line with\par euro zone peers.\par     The region's main \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened, including the Czech\par crown which shed 0.1 percent against the euro to 27.05\par by 1305 GMT even though it almost always sticks to levels closer\par to 27, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's cap which serves to prevent a firming.\par     The zloty, after hitting a 3-week low of 4.4435,\par rebounded to 4.4281, but was still down 0.3 percent from Monday,\par while the forint and the leu each shed 0.2\par percent.\par     The cost of insuring exposure to debt from some countries in\par the region, including Poland, rose to multi-week highs.\par  \par     The implied volatility of the zloty, a gauge of expected\par swings against the euro, surged to a 4-year high\par and that of the forint was at an 8-month high.\par     Investors in Poland are worried both by the risk of a\par British exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  and by the Warsaw government's policies\par for the banking sector and the rule of law.\par     "If the (British) polls favour the 'Leave' camp, we could\par reach January's lows at around 4.50," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  dealer\par Sebastian Cichy.\par     Polish central banker Jerzy Kropiwnicki said late on Monday\par the zloty's recent weakening had been caused by uncertainty over\par the British referendum, but there would be no reason to consider\par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as long as the zloty did not approach 5\par against the euro. \par     "Kropiwnicki essentially gave speculators a green light to\par bet against the Polish zloty should Brexit materialise," said\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note.\par     Polish rate setter Lukasz Hardt said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should\par not fight against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par  \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  led a fall in government bond prices in the region.\par The yield on 10-year paper surged by 10 basis\par points to 3.265, near its highest level since late January.\par     Dovish comments from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which will\par start a two-day meeting on Tuesday, could give some support to\par Central European assets, analysts said.\par     Warsaw's bluechip index fell 1.5 percent, led lower\par by energy and financial shares, stopping just short of 4-month\par lows. \par     Budapest's main index touched a 3-month low and\par Prague set a new 7-year low.    \par            CEE      SNAP   AT  1505       \par            MARKETS