\par\par \b \uc2 While others resist, Poland invites a flood of ECB cash, risks exports\b0\par\par\uc2 957 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2015\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Hungary, Romania, Serbia cut rates to dampen \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Zloty 7 pct stronger against euro this year\par \par \uc2 * Poland expected to act when zloty rise hits exports\par \par \uc2 By Marcin Goettig\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, April 27 (Reuters) - Cushioned by growth and facing elections, Poland is becoming an outlier in central Europe, where other countries are trying to curb the growing strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of the euro zone's massive money-printing plan.\par \par \uc2 Hungary, Romania and Serbia have all cut rates this year to prevent their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from gaining too much, as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's programme feeds 60 billion euros a month into the continent's economy through September 2016.\par \par \uc2 The Czech Republic has put a cap on the value of the crown and pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep it from rising\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the other hand, says it will not cut interest rates from the current 1.5 percent, even though the zloty has risen nearly 7 percent against the euro since the start of 2015 -- more than any other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And with Polish bonds providing a significant yield premium over negative or rock-bottom euro yields, more cash is likely to flood in, barring a major flare-up in neighbouring Ukraine or a Greek exit from the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 "Poland is clearly more confident than its neighbours of sustaining relatively strong growth without a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Manik Narain, a strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, all recent data show a recovering economy, which the government expects will grow at 3.4 percent this year. While inflation is currently at minus 1.5 percent, the government expects it to reach 1.7 percent by next year. The housing market, at least in Warsaw, is robust.\par \par \uc2 What's more, presidential and parliamentary elections are due this year, and a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plays well with voters enjoying cheaper vacations and imported goods, analysts note.\par \par \uc2 There are also 550,000 Poles holding mortgages denominated in Swiss francs. They saw monthly payments jump after January, when the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned its cap on the franc. A weaker zloty would exacerbate their plight, probably to the detriment of the ruling Civic Platform.\par \par \uc2 "Poland has some of the strongest cyclical numbers in terms of industrial output and retail sales, plus there is the fact that Europe's economy continues to improve. This is causing them to hold back," said Koon Chow, investment strategist at Swiss private bank UBP.\par \par \uc2 A side effect of Poland's reluctance to curtail zloty appreciation is likely to be more investment flows from funds fleeing the euro zone's negative yields -- Polish 5-year bonds yield a respectable 2.0 percent-plus.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Poland's inflation-adjusted rate of 3 percent is the highest among 23 big economies, bar Brazil and Nigeria, Reuters data shows.\par \par \uc2 NEGATIVE YIELDS\par \par \uc2 The zloty jumped 1 percent on Monday, approaching recent 3 1/2-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Chow reckons if the zloty appreciates further, Poland will have no choice but to follow its neighbours' lead and stem appreciation. A 25 bps rate cut by mid-year is likely, he says.\par \par \uc2 So far, of all the countries struggling to contain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Czech Republic has gone furthest. It cut interest rates to near zero in 2012, and in late 2013 declared it would keep the crown weaker than 27 per euro to ward off deflation.\par \par \uc2 However, the Czech economy was in recession in 2012 until early 2013. In contrast, Poland has grown robustly for the last 20 years. That makes deflation a far greater threat for Prague.\par \par \uc2 Hungary, meanwhile, resumed policy easing in March, cutting rates to a record-low 1.8 percent and signaling more ahead. Government plans to cut Hungary's high banking tax show its intention to ask banks to increase lending, analysts say. That makes low interest rates a necessity.\par \par \uc2 Romania and Serbia have also cut rates and both have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to tamp down the leu and dinar .\par \par \uc2 All that has taken some steam out of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The forint, for instance, is down about 1 percent since the April 21 rate cut.\par \par \uc2 A key motivation, of course, is trade. Exports, mostly to the euro zone, accounted for 88 percent of Hungary's annual economic output, \uc2 World Bank\uc2  data for 2013 showed. Czech exports were 77 percent of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Poland, by in contrast, with 40 million people, has a healthier domestic market, with exports comprising just 46 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Still, for some, central bankers' lack of concern is worrying, especially as the zloty's impact is already being felt among exporters. The exchange rate, currently 4 per euro, is just 3 percent off 3.91. Beyond that level, companies say, exports become unprofitable..\par \par \uc2 And business sentiment has worsened this year, with the zloty's appreciation and falling prices having "negatively affected sales margins, both export and domestic ones", according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll, which also predicted "relatively low exports dynamics" ahead.\par \par \uc2 At less than 4 zloty to the euro, "exporters will be in a difficult situation," Chow said. "Also, you can't assume European growth will remain firm. It's not an environment where monetary conditions should be tight."\par \par \uc2 Many will agree. Analysts polled by Reuters see one chance in four that policymakers would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the zloty this year. But that is probably some way off.\par \par \uc2 "We probably need to see zloty closer to 3.90 before they take a more activist approach," Narain of \uc2 UBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jason Hovet in PRAGUE and Sandor Peto in BUDAPEST and Sujata Rao in LONDON; Editing by Matt Robinson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-27T16:28:38.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150427eb4r00gwx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
