\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Unfazed by zloty gains, Poland confirms easing cycle ended\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2015\par 19:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Main interest rate held flat at 1.50 pct, as expected\par \par \uc2 * Belka sounds relaxed note on zloty gains\par \par \uc2 * Strong zloty could depress prices further (Adds details, Belka quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 By Pawel Florkiewicz and Marcin Goettig\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, April 15 (Reuters) - Poland has ended its rate-cutting cycle, its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor confirmed on Wednesday, showing little concern for a sharp strengthening of the zloty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fuelled by the euro zone's massive monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier on Wednesday decided to keep its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 1.50 percent, in line with the expectations of analysts polled by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "It would be unnatural and I would say even perhaps silly for us to start considering some changes a month after the Council decided to end the cycle, especially that nothing has happened that would surprise us," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Marek Belka\uc2  told a news conference after the rate decision.\par \par \uc2 Belka sounded relaxed about the zloty, which tested the psychological level of 4.00 against the euro earlier this week, its strongest level since late 2011.\par \par \uc2 "We note with satisfaction that the exchange rate in Poland moves freely ... The free float is working, sometimes it's up, sometimes it's down. I've got nothing else to say," Belka said.\par \par \uc2 The zloty has gained about 7 percent versus the euro since the start of the year and about 10 percent since late December. However, it has weakened against the dollar and the Swiss franc compared with last year.\par \par \uc2 Polish rates are currently at an all-time low but are still above those of the euro zone, where they are near to zero.\par \par \uc2 Sharp zloty gains versus the euro, combined with the bank's announcement of an end to rate cuts, have led analysts to consider the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of analysts showed on Tuesday they see about one chance in four that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the zloty this year.\par \par \uc2 FALLING PRICES\par \par \uc2 Inflation in Poland stood at minus 1.5 percent year-on-year in March, far below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plus 2.5 percent target. A stronger zloty could depress prices further.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Belka said a stronger zloty could trigger a spiral of deflation and economic weakness but that the likelihood was low.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from the March meeting showed last month a majority of policymakers thought the announcement of ending rate cuts would not prevent them from adjusting rates if the economy suffered a "strong shock".\par \par \uc2 Asked on Wednesday whether sharp zloty gains would qualify as a "strong shock", Belka said a sudden and significant slowdown in economic growth could fit such a description.\par \par \uc2 Belka also said the bank would not be making any payments into the state budget from the profits on its 2014 \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Additional reporting by Pawel Sobczak and Jakub Iglewski; Writing by Marcin Goettig; Editing by Gareth Jones) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-15T18:03:16.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150415eb4f00nzz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s