\par\par \b \uc2 Polish Finance Minister Says Zloty's Standing Doesn't Harm Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2015\par 16:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Martin M. Sobczyk \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WARSAW--Poland's economy can tolerate the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent rise to the euro, even if it does pose a challenge for local exporters, said the finance minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The zloty's strengthening, more than 8% to the euro since the beginning of the year, isn't a concern because economic growth in Poland is driven by investment and consumption rather than exports, said Mateusz Szczurek on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "It is a factor that reduces the profitability of exports," he said. "But I'm convinced that at these levels it's not harmful to Polish companies."\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was around 4.02 zlotys to the euro on Thursday afternoon after nearing 4.00 a day earlier, a psychological level.\par \par \uc2 The local unit of Germany's \uc2 Commerzbank AG\uc2 , \uc2 mBank SA\uc2 , said in a research note that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has room to rise further by some 5%, to the region of 3.85 zlotys to the euro, supported by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond-buying program as well as Poland's solid economic performance and real interest rates above those in the eurozone.\par \par \uc2 The zloty was at its strongest since mid-2011 on Wednesday, prompting talk among economists of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has a history of stepping in when it believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves are too rapid.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has over the past years \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly to strengthen the zloty at times of global market turmoil. Governor, \uc2 Marek Belka\uc2 , said in March, however, that the bank has "practically forgotten" about \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the zloty.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Szczurek said Thursday that any rapid moves of the zloty complicate doing business for Poland, which ships most of its exports to the eurozone.\par \par \uc2 "But Polish companies have for a long time been operating in the floating exchange rate regime. We have seen examples of even bigger moves in this or that direction. The Polish economy has weathered bigger swings than this," Mr. Szczurek said of the zloty's rise this year.\par \par \uc2 Any exchange rate below 4 zlotys to the euro has over the past decade been considered strong for the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which peaked in strength around 3.20 to the euro in 2008, just before the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 . In the aftermath of the global financial crisis, the zloty tumbled quickly, trading above 4.90 to the euro in early 2009.\par \par \uc2 Write to Martin M. Sobczyk at Martin.Sobczyk@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 09, 2015 10:51 ET (14:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150409eb49002cp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
