\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss Move Shows  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Can't Operate in Isolation\b0\par\par\uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2015\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's game-altering move to end the Swiss franc's ceiling against the euro creates challenges for its European counterparts, while traders are given a new safe haven option.\par \par \uc2 The lesson: No \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an island.\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, when the SNB capped the Swiss franc's value against the euro, it diminished the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's traditional role as a parking space when other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were in trouble. That limited the options for a stampede of investors leaving the euro and later became an issue for those fleeing the Russian ruble.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's historical reputation for financial stability -- and the fact that it was an advanced European economy outside the \uc2 European Union\uc2  -- kept a modest money flow going there. But without the prospect of exchange gains, many money managers went elsewhere. Some went to traditional havens such as the dollar or the yen, others chased European alternatives like the Polish zloty, Swedish krona or Czech koruna. Those smaller central banks found themselves in the middle of a battle between two bigger players, the SNB and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The latest development once again puts them in the crossfire.\par \par \uc2 In ending the 1.20 Swiss-franc floor at which it vowed to buy euros, the SNB blasted a dam and unleashed a monetary flood. Most eyes are watching where that flow is going: into the Swiss franc, which has soared versus the euro. But it's also worth thinking about where it's coming from. The euro is the obvious loser, but consider the zloty: Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately dropped 21% against the franc in the aftermath, but also 2.2% against the dollar and 1.45% against the euro.\par \par \uc2 In truth, both Poland's and Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been weakening against the euro well before the SNB's latest move, which is saying something, given that the euro is down around 15% versus the dollar over the past 12 months. But their declines were an indirect result of the SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which forced euro sellers into their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That initially led to overvaluations, and prompted those countries' central banks to tamp them down. The Swedish Riksbank slashed rates to zero and revisited the idea of negative rates it had first used during the global financial crisis. The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  employed a periodic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to push the zloty lower. Markets eventually got the message, but not before inflation in both countries, as well as the Czech Republic, was headed inexorably toward zero.\par \par \uc2 Now, these central banks must suddenly return to the drawing board. On Wednesday, \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it was considering cutting rates, a suggestion that it still thought the zloty was too strong. A day later it's worried about a plummeting zloty. Polish rate cuts are likely off the table for now.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from this is a reminder that central banks never operate in a bubble -- not the \uc2 National Bank of Poland\uc2 , not the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , not even the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the U.S. Federal Reserve, though it's always true that the actions of the bigger bank impact the smaller one more than the other way around.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the SNB policies over the past three years are direct responses to those of a bigger bank. The ECB's struggle to, first, resolve the eurozone debt crisis and, then, to contain the regional economic deterioration, drove a steady flow of euros into francs. If that flow had been allowed to boost the franc's valuation, it would have hurt Swiss exporters -- hence the SNB's imposition of a euro price floor. Ultimately, though, it was again the ECB that foiled the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy. In hinting at a big new bond-buying, or quantitative easing, program, the ECB drove a new, large influx of euros into francs, so many that its Swiss counterpart could no longer keep buying them to maintain the CHF1.20 rate.\par \par \uc2 Today's move "reflects the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordination" and the need for it, says Larry Goodman, President of the Center for Financial Stability in New York. "It shows how \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies radiate from the Fed to the ECB to the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 -- Follow Michael J. Casey on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @mikejcasey.\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Schweizerische Nationalbank\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=CH0001319265"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=CH0001319265}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-01-15 0138GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150116eb1g00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
