\par\par \b \uc2 *Poland Reserved About Euro Adoption -- Finance Minister\b0\par\par\uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2014\par 16:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 27 May 2014 10:30 ET *Poland Needs More Time to Get Ready for Euro -- Finance Minister\par \par \uc2 27 May 2014 10:30 ET *Poland Needs Lower Public Debt to Join Euro Area -- Finance Minister\par \par \uc2 27 May 2014 10:30 ET *Poland's Finance Minister Szczurek Comments from WSJ Interview\par \par \uc2 27 May 2014 10:30 ET  Poland Remains Reserved on Euro Adoption, Says Finance Minister\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Patryk Wasilewski \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WARSAW--Poland remains reserved about adopting the euro, its finance minister said in an interview Tuesday, despite suggestions that euro-zone membership could offer added security amid concerns about potential Russian aggression.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mateusz Szczurek said Tuesday that Poland would consider joining the European single-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area when the country thinks the move will benefit the economy and won't threaten its financial stability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Indeed in relation to the Ukrainian crisis there are voices in Poland and in the [Central European] region that for security and political reasons a quicker euro entry is needed," Mr. Szczurek said. "Our view is unchanged. Euro entry can improve our security and guarantee us a bigger impact on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policies only in tandem with our growing economic strength."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The conflict between Russia and Ukraine has made Poland and others in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's east nervous that they could potentially become targets of Russian aggression in the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid those concerns, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Marek Belka said in March that the conflict adds another element to the discussion about adopting the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Romania recently set its euro-entry target for 2019.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland originally planned to replace the zloty with the euro in 2012 but abandoned the target when it failed to meet criteria for candidates during the global financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Szczurek said Poland needs to bridge the wealth divide that separates it from the more affluent EU nations before setting a new target date for adopting the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro-zone debt crisis demonstrated that membership of the bloc alone doesn't strengthen the economy, Mr. Szczurek said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the bloc struggling to grow and some of the euro zone's members suffering financial problems, support for the euro in Poland has waned, with recent TNS OBOP opinion poll showing only a small minority now supporting accession over the next 10 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Opponents argue that Poland is already taking full advantage of being part of the EU and that retaining its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allows the economy to absorb shocks and increase competitiveness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Without a floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , one of the few tools at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's disposal to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the economy, the country would need to increase public spending and deficits at times of downturn. To create room for such actions after joining the euro, Poland will have to cut its public debt to around 40% of gross domestic product, Mr. Szczurek said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public debt is around 50% of GDP and the country continues to run fiscal deficits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Increased government spending at the height of the global financial crisis in 2009, as well as a steady stream of EU funds into the country, made Poland the only EU member to avoid a recession that year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a period of slow economic growth, Poland's economy is now recovering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Szczurek said growth this year could top the ministry's forecast of 3.3% GDP growth, with the public finance deficit "very likely" narrowing to 3.5% of GDP from 4.3% in 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another obstacle Poland faces on its path toward euro-area membership is the requirement for candidates to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a relatively narrow corridor to the euro for two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor previously said this would lead to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and pressure on the zloty to strengthen, which would reduce Poland's competitiveness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Szczurek echoed those concerns in the interview, saying that some of the requirements to join the euro area, such as keeping a lid on public spending, "are worth pursuing even without the plan to join the euro zone" but that keeping the zloty in the pre-euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  grid isn't a good idea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland will also likely wait before joining a proposed banking union for the EU, Mr. Szczurek said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's policy makers have said the European banking regulator may prove to be less prudent than its domestic counterpart, whose conservative approach is seen as one of the reasons why Poland's banking sector weathered the global financial crisis well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland also has its own bank-deposit guarantee program financed by the banks themselves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our domestic banking sector, regulator and guarantee systems are too good to risk changing them without making sure how the European mechanisms work in practice," Mr. Szczurek said. "However, we are interested in the success of the banking union because a strong and stable banking system in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  is beneficial to Poland."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Patryk Wasilewski at patryk.wasilewski@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 27, 2014 10:30 ET (14:30 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140527ea5r0024r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
