\par\par \b \uc2 Polish  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Keeps Benchmark Rate Unchanged--Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Marcin Sobczyk   \par \uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 18:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW-\uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday extended its informal neutral bias faced with subdued price growth, a moderate economic recovery and the Russian-Ukrainian conflict, the bank's governor said.\par \par \uc2 The bank left the benchmark rate unchanged at 2.5%, the lowest on record, and said borrowing costs would remain unchanged until at least the end of the third quarter of this year and possibly even longer. \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  previously said rates would remain on hold until at least the end of the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 Poland should have very low consumer price inflation for longer than previously expected, said Marek Belka, head of \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank said CPI would likely be near the 2.5% target some time in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The Russian-Ukrainian conflict and what \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees as an armed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Crimea accelerated the extension of the \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's neutral stance on rates.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in Ukraine sped up our decision to show that we don't see any threats for the economy, at least as to monetary policy," Mr. Belka said. "We wanted to show our confidence in the strength of the economy's fundamentals and the zloty."\par \par \uc2 Following Russia's actions in Ukraine, Mr. Belka said this week Poland could reconsider its stalled euro-zone membership bid to integrate even more closely with the rest of Europe.\par \par \uc2 By joining the euro area, the core of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , Poland would be able to shield itself better from any turmoil from outside the bloc, Mr. Belka said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "When you're a euro zone member, the situation in the country more directly impacts other euro-zone countries. Imagine Italy's economic problems 30 years ago-would Germany or France lament over them?" he said.\par \par \uc2 "Today what's going on in one large euro-zone country directly impacts the economic stability of other countries. That is the point of entering the single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area," he added.\par \par \uc2 In economic terms, risks are huge and benefits more modest than they appeared previously, Mr. Belka also said. Poland's current government originally planned for the country to join the euro zone in 2012 but abandoned the plan amid the global financial crisis. In recent months, officials have been reserved about the membership prospect.\par \par \uc2 The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly reacted to heightened tensions between Russia and Ukraine this week, moving near 4.22 zlotys to the euro on Monday. On Wednesday afternoon, it was back near 4.18, stable on the day.\par \par \uc2 Sharing land borders with Russia and Ukraine, Poland has encouraged its \uc2 European Union\uc2  and \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  allies to stand up to the Kremlin. One of the main reasons behind its push in the 1990s to join Western European economic and political organizations was hope that even a resurgent Russia would be less likely to challenge Poland as part of a larger alliance.\par \par \uc2 Write to Marcin Sobczyk at marcin.sobczyk@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 05, 2014 12:11 ET (17:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140305ea350033f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
