\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Forint falls on rate uncertainty, Romania props up leu\b0\par\par\uc2 1,059 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2014\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds new comments, prices)\par     * Forint hovers near 2-year lows, rate outlook uncertain\par     * Leu bucks trend after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says would worry more about zloty gains than falls\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Jan 30 (Reuters) - The forint fell to two-year\par lows against the euro on Thursday, extending losses after the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suggested it could cut interest rates again and as\par a further sell-off in emerging markets hit Central Europe's most\par liquid assets.\par     The leu bucked the regional trend after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to provide support in its first\par such move this year, dealers said. \par     A rout in emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has started to spread\par to Central Europe, which had previously held up relatively well\par compared to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Turkish lira or the South\par African rand, because the region was supported by better\par economic fundamentals.\par     The forint has seen the biggest hit, which\par accelerated on Wednesday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Gyorgy\par Matolcsy said low inflation left some room for the bank to cut\par its base rate further from a record low 2.85 percent.\par     That prospect made investors nervous, contrasting with\par rising Hungarian bond yields and rate hikes in several other\par emerging markets.\par     The forint fell against the euro on Thursday to levels\par unseen since early 2012. It rebounded from technical support\par levels at 312.50 three times during the session, and at 1526 GMT\par it was bid at 310.41, still down 0.6 percent from Wednesday.\par     The forint is by far central Europe's worst-performing\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year, with losses of more than 4 percent.\par     Government bond prices recovered from sharp early falls.\par     The yield at a 12-month Treasury bill auction jumped to 3.51\par percent from 2.84 percent two weeks ago. Forward rate agreements\par also signalled uncertainty over interest rates, pricing in 55\par basis points worth of rate hikes for the next 3 months.\par     Market participants said further fast falls in the forint\par could make the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or halt its rate\par cuts, but any bets that it could be forced into reversing\par monetary easing stood little chance of materialising.\par     "They (investors) single out the forint instinctively but\par they forget that this market is not what it used to be a few\par years back," one Budapest-based dealer said.  \par     "They cannot force the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into rate hikes," the\par dealer added. "The current account is massively in surplus,\par there is a huge inflow of euros from EU funds, so even if they\par try they will get nowhere."\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Benoit Anne said in a note that\par hedge funds which pushed the Turkish and South African central\par banks into interest rate hikes could do the same in other\par emerging markets including Hungary. \par     "I have quite a bit more faith in the NBP (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in\par Poland, but I would also like to point out that the PLN (zloty)\par is quite expensive relative to its peers and therefore\par constitutes a prime target," he added.\par    \par     POLAND PLAYS DOWN CONTAGION\par     Hungarian markets have been worst hit in the region because\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headed by Matolcsy, a key ally of Prime\par Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viktor Orban\f28 \uc2 \f2  who is seeking re-election in April, has\par been aggressively cutting rates from 7 percent in 2012.   \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already finished its own rate\par cuts and Poland has better debt ratings than junk-rated Hungary.\par But the zloty is still vulnerable to emerging market contagion\par as the region's most liquid unit, making it easy to sell.\par     The zloty also rebounded against the euro in the\par afternoon. It was weaker by 0.4 percent from Wednesday, but at\par 4.232 it is still far from 4.35, the level where the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last year.\par     "The current EM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakening is chiefly of a\par speculative character," one Warsaw-based dealer said. "So a time\par will come when the speculators will be booking profits and then\par the zloty should rebound thanks to its strong fundamentals."\par     Polish Finance Minister Mateusz Szczurek said the zloty's 2\par percent weakening since last Thursday was no concern and it was\par more worrying that strong economic fundamentals could lead to a\par strengthening of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .     \par     The leu firmed 0.6 percent to 4.508 per euro after\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romania's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought it in the market through\par commercial banks to defend it, dealers said. ING said in a note\par that "4.50/EUR looks like a close target on the near-run radar."\par     One dealer in Bucharest said: "It was a good moment to step\par in indirectly. The amount sold is pretty large."\par                       CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1626 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.561    27.534   -0.10%  -0.83%\par  Hungarian forint           310.410   308.600   -0.58%  -4.33%\par  Polish zloty                 4.232     4.217    -0.35%  -1.94%\par  Romanian leu                 4.508     4.527  +0.43%   -1.06%\par  Croatian kuna                7.660     7.647    -0.17%  -0.55%\par  Serbian dinar              115.870   115.770    -0.09%   -1.18%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\par  Prague                      994.43    990.63  +0.38% +0.76%\par  Budapest                  18652.91  18546.80   +0.57%  +1.74%\par  Warsaw                     2320.50   2313.34   +0.31%  -3.49%\par  Bucharest                  6391.54   6446.84   -0.86%   -1.33%\par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                          Yield    Yield    Spread    Daily\par                          (