\par\par \b \uc2 Polish Rate Setter Zielinska: Zloty Secure, Economy Safe and Sound\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patryk Wasilewski   \par \uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2014\par 14:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW--Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safeguarded from market turbulence despite its current weakness because the country's economy is viewed as stable and it will grow robustly again this year, while monetary policy is conservative, a central banker said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Some emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated this week as investors sold riskier assets and sought the safety of U.S. papers amid fears about the robustness of the global economy's recovery.\par \par \uc2 Tracking the euro's decline against the U.S. dollar, the Polish zloty Friday hit a four-month low against the euro and traded at 4.22 at around noon local time. The Hungarian forint traded at its weakest level since March 2013, around 306 forints to the euro after Hungry's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week cut its benchmark rate to 2.85%, a level many economists consider excessively low for the economy.\par \par \uc2 The Czech koruna was broadly stable Friday at its weakest level since 2009, where it has been since early November when the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and spur inflation and growth.\par \par \uc2 The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be supported by Poland's relatively resilient economy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's conservative policy, Anna Zielinska-Glebocka told The Wall Street Journal. The benchmark rate in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's largest emerging economy is 2.5%, despite a series of cuts in 2012-2013 it remains significantly above the euro zone's 0.25% or neighboring Czech Republic's 0.05%.\par \par \uc2 "For the time being, no risks for the zloty are in sight and let it remain so," Mrs. Zielinksa-Glebocka.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the local market several times since 2011 to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at times when it said volatility and the pace of weakening was excessive.\par \par \uc2 "[We] won't start engaging in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars [now]," Mrs. Zielinska-Glebocka said. "Although of course we monitor the spread between interest rates in Poland and for example the euro zone."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor, Marek Belka, has repeatedly said the \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at all times ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prevent any rapid changes to the zloty exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The 10-strong rate panel in January reiterated interest rates will remain unchanged at least until June.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly our next move will be to increase interest rates, but when that happens will depend on the forecasts due in March," Mrs. Zielinska-Glebocka said. "At the moment it is really difficult to say when monetary policy tightening will begin."\par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases its official inflation and growth projections every four months. In its latest projections in November, it forecast growth at 2.9% and inflation at 1.7% this year. However, Mrs. Zielinska-Glebocka said growth will likely be higher than 3%.\par \par \uc2 At this rate, Poland's growth would be faster than the respective \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts of 1.5% for the Czech economy and 2.1% for Hungary.\par \par \uc2 "It is possible that annual growth will be at 3.5%-3.8% in particular quarters," Mrs. Zielinska-Glebocka said.\par \par \uc2 Poland's economy will in turn once again grow relatively fast after a slump last year, when it grew only 1.3%, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one of the worst figures for the past two decades.\par \par \uc2 Polish CPI could reach the bank's 2.5% target but only in 2015 and will likely be lower than projected in November, she said.\par \par \uc2 This may mean the \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep rates on hold beyond the first half of this year and it's not certain borrowing costs will go up in 2014, Mrs. Zielinska-Glebocka said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Patryk Wasilewski at patryk.wasilewski@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 24, 2014 08:11 ET (13:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140124ea1o001ia\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
