\par\par \b \uc2 CEE Morning Briefing: Czech President Swears In New Premier\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2014\par 03:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 1000/0900 Czech current account balance in Nov. \par 1400/1300 Polish current account balance in Nov. \par 1500/1400 Czech President swears in new Prime Minister \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nearly three months after elections, Bohuslav Sobotka of the Social Democratic Party takes office Friday as prime minister of the Czech Republic, replacing a caretaker government that has run the country since August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Social Democrats have teamed up with two other parties--the Centrist Action of Dissatisfied Citizens and the center-left Christian Democrats--to form their first government since 2006.\par \par \uc2 The governing coalition commands 111 seats in the 200-strong lower house elected in October. A corruption scandal brought down the previous conservative cabinet in June.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the Czech and Polish central banks will release current account balances for November.\par \par \uc2 The Czechs are likely to post a slightly smaller surplus because \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November have weakened the koruna versus the euro.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account gap should widen, with imports rising faster than exports for a first time in months. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0250 GMT 27.487-525 \par Previous 2150 GMT 27.483-519 \par %Chg +0.02 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0250 GMT 300.40-84 \par Previous 2150 GMT 300.29-88 \par % Chg  +0.01 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0250 GMT 4.1649-724 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.1643-713 \par % Chg  +0.02 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par          Thurs  Wed \par 3 yrs    3.65%  3.70% \par 5 yrs    4.62%  4.64% \par 10yrs    5.44%  5.44% \par  \par Poland \par          Thurs  Wed \par 3 yrs    2.91%  2.93% \par 5 yrs    3.55%  3.60% \par 10yrs    4.35%  4.40% \par  \par Czech Republic \par          Thurs  Wed \par 3 yrs    0.55%  0.56% \par 5 yrs    1.17%  1.24% \par 10yrs    2.42%  2.45% \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,401.87+5.19+0.22% \par  \par BUX \par 19,459.58+36.56+0.19% \par  \par PX \par 1,025.75-9.97-0.96% \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's top bank by assets \f28 \uc2 \f2 Powszechna Kasa Oszczednosci Bank Polski S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (PKO.WA) sold bonds worth EUR500 million ($681.5 million) Thursday, after it reopened its five-year eurobond issue amid high demand from financial investors. The bank said the issue was priced 115 basis points above mid swap level, despite originally aiming for a spread of 130bps, the bank added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intends to keep the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaker at just above 27 korunas to the euro through the end of the year, but after that the bank won't prevent it from strengthening, the bank's governor said late Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agrofert\f28 \uc2 \f2  AS, a Czech fertilizer and food maker owned by the country's incoming finance minister, is preparing to bid for the Croatian chemicals company Petrokemija d.d.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're seriously interested in Petrokemija and we've submitted a letter of intent to the Croatian authorities," Agrofert spokesman Karel Hanzelka said without elaborating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary is enjoying an increasingly positive business relationship with Moscow, a senior government official said Thursday, days after the country awarded a contract to expand its sole nuclear plant to a Russian firm, going against wider European attempts to reduce reliance on Russian energy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Patryk Wasilewski in Warsaw contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 \f2  for the WSJ blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 16, 2014 21:53 ET (02:53 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140117ea1h0002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
