\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Polish yields fall, Prague stocks shine on easy Fed outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2013\par 14:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Polish 10-year yield hits 9-week low on rate outlook\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Prague stocks at 5-month high on global sentiment\par     * Expectations grow that U.S. Fed stimulus to stay longer\par     By Marton Dunai and Jason Hovet\par     BUDAPEST/PRAGUE, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Polish 10-year bond\par yields hit a nine-week low on Friday on expectations that\par interest rates would stay at record lows well into next year,\par while Prague stocks rallied to a five-month high.\par     Investors' appetite for riskier emerging market assets has\par been boosted by expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve will keep\par its stimulus in place for longer following the American\par government shutdown.\par     In Hungary, the prospect of more interest rate cuts was\par likely to put new pressure on the forint, while the crown crept\par back to a three-week low as the threat of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weighed on the market. \par     Analysts say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's interest rate is likely to stay\par unchanged at 2.50 percent as the economy starts to pick up from\par a slowdown, helping bonds rally. The 10-year yield fell to 4.20\par percent. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policymaker Elzbieta Chojna-Duch said on\par Thursday that rates should stay low until the middle of next\par year as the economic revival was still too weak to create\par inflationary pressures. \par     "The signals from Polish bankers support (bonds). Investors\par forecast that we will see the first interest rate hikes in Q3 of\par 2014," Millennium Bank analyst Mateusz Sutowicz said.\par     "Moreover, due to the government shutdown in the U.S., the\par Fed should be less likely to taper its QE3 programme this year."\par     The Fed's stimulus has sent cheap money into emerging\par markets, buoying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region at a time when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been cutting rates to boost slumping economies.\par     Serbia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut its main interest rate by 50 basis\par points to 10.5 percent on Friday, its first cut since June. \par    The bank said it had been able to cut its benchmark rate,\par which is still the highest in the region, because its policies,\par combined with low agricultural prices and weak demand had helped\par bring down inflation. \par     The dinar was little moved at 113.90 per euro.\par     Erste Bank analysts wrote in a note that there was a risk of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the forint - already near a three-month high against the euro -weakening as markets weighed the Hungarian government's plans to reduce mortgage payments for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowers and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's drive to reduce interest rates further. The forint was a touch up at 294.0 per euro, while the Czech crown lost 0.3 percent to 25.764. The Polish zloty lost 0.4 percent and Romania's leu dipped 0.1 percent. The Prague bourse led gains among emerging European stock markets. "The mood is driven by the positive sentiment on global markets," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Josef Nemy said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Central European Media Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CME) rose 5 percent in Prague after its shares jumped on the Nasdaq in the previous session. The company, fighting slumping advertising markets, announced the departure of its chief financial officer on Thursday as part of a shuffle at the group where major shareholder Time Warner has been strengthening its hold. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1403 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2013\par  Czech crown                 25.764    25.680   -0.33%  -2.78%\par  Hungarian forint           294.000   294.420  +0.14%  -1.03%\par  Polish zloty                 4.180     4.166    -0.35%  -2.54%\par  Romanian leu                 4.449     4.445   -0.09%   -0.13%\par  Croatian kuna                7.612     7.614   +0.02%  -0.80%\par  Serbian dinar              113.900   113.810    -0.08%   -1.40%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2013\par  Prague                      987.00    979.27  +0.79%  -4.98%\par  Budapest                  18530.79  18456.14   +0.40%  +1.97%\par  Warsaw                     2488.91   2474.92   +0.57%  -3.64%\par  Bucharest                  5966.80   6032.99   -1.10%  +15.87%\par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                          Yield    Yield    Spread    Daily\par                          (bid)    change   vs Bund   change in\par  Czech Republic                                      spread\par    2-year                0.185    -0.056   +1bps     -5bps\par    5-year                1.203     -0.004   +39bps     +3bps\par   10-year                2.444    -0.027   +62bps    +1bps\par  Poland\par    2-year                3.020    -0.03