\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  gain, central banks in focus\b0\par\par\uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2013\par 17:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Zloty leads Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up weak zloty\par     * Czech, Hungarian central banks meet this week\par  (Updates markets, adds stocks, bonds)\par     WARSAW/BUDAPEST, June 24 (Reuters) - Central European\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded stronger on Monday led by the previously battered Polish zloty, with investors anticipating further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slides again. The zloty, the region's most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was up half a percent against the euro by 1437 GMT, the Hungarian forint gained 0.4 percent, the Romanian leu a third of a percent and the Czech crown 0.1 percent. "We are at such weak (zloty) levels and depreciation has been so fast that there could be more and more comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  about a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to scare the market," said one Warsaw-based dealer. The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed around 5.3 percent in two months to hover around one-year lows. In response, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 7 for the first time in 18 months, and dealers think it may do so again. The Hungarian and the Czech central banks hold policy meetings this week against a backdrop of generally poor sentiment toward emerging market assets. \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to cut rates again on Tuesday but may express concerns that the emerging markets sell-off has hit forint assets and signal that the bank's easing cycle could end soon, a Reuters poll showed. "They are expected by the market to cut this one time by another 25 basis points, then take a breather," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said in Budapest. The forint, which is usually among the more volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, could recoup some of its recent losses once the rate cut is executed, he said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains happened despite a still-nervous investor mood highlighted by bond yields rising by about 40 basis points in Hungary and a mixed performance on stock markets. Prague stocks fell 2.5 percent to an 18-month low, dragged down by Erste Bank shares, while indexes in Budapest and Warsaw were little changed. Czech investors were eyeing political developments as well as Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting. On Tuesday, President Milos Zeman is due to announce a new prime minister and has hinted at forming a technocrat cabinet to replace the government of Petr Necas, who resigned last week in a bribery and spying scandal involving his aide. The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not expected to change interest rates that are already near zero. It has threatened to knock the crown lower if it needs to ease policy further but a Reuters poll showed it is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now as parts of the economy have begun to show signs of recovery. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPS